===== CALL rc=0 out=687B args=eval (() => { const t=document.querySelector('#mw-content-text').innerText; const idx=t.search(/billion years ago|Gya|Ga ago|4\.[0-9] billion/i); return t.slice(Math.max(0,idx-300), idx+300); })() =====
result: "\", is an astrogeology hypothesis for the formation of the Moon first proposed in 1946 by Canadian geologist Reginald Daly. The hypothesis suggests that the Proto-Earth collided with a Mars-sized co-orbital protoplanet likely from the L4 or L5 Lagrange points of the Earth's orbit[3] approximately 4.5 billion years ago in the early Hadean eon (about 20 to 100 million years after the Solar System formed), and some of the ejected debris from the impact event later re-accreted to form the Moon.[4] The impactor planet is sometimes called Theia, named after the mythical Greek Titan who was the mother \""
help[1]:
  Run `opera-browser-cli snapshot` to see current page state
